  "name": "24 HR URINARY CHROMIUM",
  "code": "1060",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"123\" data-end=\"354\">The <strong data-start=\"127\" data-end=\"155\">24-hour urinary chromium</strong> test measures the total amount of chromium excreted in the urine over a 24-hour period. It provides a more accurate assessment of <strong data-start=\"286\" data-end=\"314\">recent chromium exposure</strong> compared to spot urine or serum levels.</p>\n<p data-start=\"356\" data-end=\"382\">It is clinically used for:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"384\" data-end=\"792\">\n<li data-start=\"384\" data-end=\"479\">\n<p data-start=\"386\" data-end=\"479\"><strong data-start=\"386\" data-end=\"422\">Monitoring occupational exposure</strong> to chromium, particularly <strong data-start=\"449\" data-end=\"479\">hexavalent chromium (Cr⁶⁺)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"480\" data-end=\"611\">\n<p data-start=\"482\" data-end=\"611\"><strong data-start=\"482\" data-end=\"524\">Evaluating potential chromium toxicity</strong> from environmental sources or <strong data-start=\"555\" data-end=\"573\">metal implants</strong> (e.g., metal-on-metal hip prostheses)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"612\" data-end=\"710\">\n<p data-start=\"614\" data-end=\"710\"><strong data-start=\"614\" data-end=\"648\">Assessing over-supplementation</strong> in individuals taking chromium-containing dietary supplements</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"711\" data-end=\"792\">\n<p data-start=\"713\" data-end=\"792\">Monitoring <strong data-start=\"724\" data-end=\"760\">patients on parenteral nutrition</strong> to ensure trace element balance</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Cause / Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Insight</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Occupational exposure</strong></td>\n<td>Workers in metal plating, welding, dye production, or tanning may show elevated chromium excretion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metal-on-metal prosthetic implants</strong></td>\n<td>Corrosion may cause systemic chromium absorption, reflected in urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High dietary or supplemental intake</strong></td>\n<td>Excessive use of chromium picolinate or similar supplements can elevate levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute environmental exposure</strong></td>\n<td>Inhalation or ingestion from contaminated water or soil</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
